Full spec, architecture, and functional requirements live in `AGENTS.md` at the repo root — read it before starting any task in this repo. This file is the short, always-loaded version of the hard constraints only.

# Stack (do not substitute)
Python 3.11+, Streamlit, LangChain, ChromaDB (local persistent), sentence-transformers
(all-MiniLM-L6-v2 embeddings + cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 reranker), rank_bm25,
pdfplumber/pypdf, Anthropic `anthropic` SDK, python-dotenv. No FastAPI, no Docker, no auth,
no new dependency without asking first.

# Build order
utils.py -> pdf_loader.py -> chunking.py -> vectorstore.py -> retrieval.py -> agent.py -> app.py.
Each src/ module ships as signatures + docstrings only until implemented one at a time.
Test a module directly before wiring it into app.py.

# Hard "never" list
- Never fabricate a working function — every shipped function must run against the real PDF,
  the real Chroma store, and the real Claude API. No hardcoded/mock return values "for now."
- Never use `except: pass` or swallow an exception silently — log it, and surface user-facing
  errors in the Streamlit UI with a specific message, never a raw traceback.
- Never leave `# TODO: implement later` in code you present as finished.
- Never skip the citation-faithfulness check described in AGENTS.md §3 — if the LLM cites a
  page that wasn't actually retrieved, that citation must be stripped and logged, not trusted.
- Never add auth, multi-tenancy, cloud deployment, or CI/CD scaffolding — out of scope.
- Never hand-roll a PDF parser, text splitter, or vector index when the chosen libraries do it.
- Never import a package without verifying it's installed (`pip show <package>`) and listed in
  requirements.txt first.
- Never claim a feature works without actually running it.
- Never delete or overwrite `.env`, `requirements.txt`, or anything under `data/` without
  explicit user confirmation.
- Never pad comments/docstrings/README with restating-the-obvious text or marketing language.

# Process
Before any multi-file refactor: `git add -A && git commit -m "checkpoint: before <task>"`.
For nontrivial new modules: propose the function signature + docstring first, confirm, then
implement. Be concise — don't narrate standard code patterns, only explain non-obvious calls.
